From: RG DS OS <rgds@localhost>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 13:30:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] drm/rockchip: dsi: let the command line probe it asynchronously

The DSI driver forces a synchronous probe because, with a dual-channel display, one DSI reads the other's driver
data. The RG DS drives two separate panels, one per DSI, and the panels' 650 ms power-up (their init sequences and
reset delays) runs inside the DRM master's bind, which this probe triggers as the last component: it held the PMIC,
and so the card and the root filesystem, behind it on the boot's only thread. With the default probe strategy the
kernel's driver_async_probe=dw-mipi-dsi-rockchip opts in (extlinux.conf), and nothing changes for anyone else.
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/rockchip/dw-mipi-dsi-rockchip.c | 6 ------
 1 file changed, 6 deletions(-)

--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/rockchip/dw-mipi-dsi-rockchip.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/rockchip/dw-mipi-dsi-rockchip.c
@@ -1749,11 +1749,5 @@ struct platform_driver dw_mipi_dsi_rockchip_driver = {
 		.of_match_table = dw_mipi_dsi_rockchip_dt_ids,
 		.pm	= &dw_mipi_dsi_rockchip_pm_ops,
 		.name	= "dw-mipi-dsi-rockchip",
-		/*
-		 * For dual-DSI display, one DSI pokes at the other DSI's
-		 * drvdata in dw_mipi_dsi_rockchip_find_second(). This is not
-		 * safe for asynchronous probe.
-		 */
-		.probe_type = PROBE_FORCE_SYNCHRONOUS,
 	},
 };
